 is why you're seeing it becoming  more embedded into our strategy and  number three is consumer experience and  that's the other one you know how do we  really engage the consumer into this  equation we actually have been doing  telemedicine for several years we  actually started pilots in California  very early on what's different this time  is we're thinking about going back to  what I talked about earlier it's not  just about having a telemedicine  capability it's about how does it tie  into and so when we're doing our work  with live health online now it's very  much focused on how's it integrated with  my benefits so that it's not something I  go and have to pay out of my pocket but  it's integrated with my benefit solution  how do we make sure it's more that  experience many of the telemedicine type  scenarios you place a phone call ours is  very immersive online doctor visits much  more consumer experience when we bring  up that telemedicine visit with life  health online  as my patient record so we're really  trying to connect all the pieces  together which is now while you're  hearing us talk more about it because  it's more directly aligned with our  strategy well it sounds like all so what  you're saying is that to do something  transformative is not something where  you snap your fingers and suddenly  everything has changed yeah one of the  things and in telemedicine is a great  one a lot of times companies look at  that because it's a capability I can  sell to an employer the way I think  about it is if it doesn't work for the  consumer and the providers the the  solutions not going to work for the  long-term so in that scenario absolutely  we had to think and understand how do  you make it work so the doctors will be  available online and so when you think  about the payment innovation we talked  about earlier where we're supporting  them through reimbursement to do these  kind of visits you know that's an  important important factor if I as a  consumer go into this model and I don't  have a great experience again it won't  matter  you know in the end if you can't make  the whole system work for employers for  consumers for providers the other thing  I would say is we as we look at  innovation I would say that kind of more  generally we try to bucket it there's  normal innovation that happens in your  product lifecycle you all we always have  to think about how do you get new  products to market how do you how do you  do process improvement that's one type  we also try to look at innovation from  the context of how does it drive  long-term value and how is it tie back  to your strategy that's really important  there's some game changers and I would  put Watson in that game change your  bucket but we have to focus on just a  few of those and there's other  opportunities where you can partner with  a company such as what we're doing with  live health online for the telemedicine  type solution and then there's also  companies we put an investment in so we  can get first to market on new  capabilities so we really try to look at  the various forms of innovation and  understand you know you can only make a  few big bets but you can do innovation  in many other ways embedded throughout  your business and drive a lot of value  to your company so what would you say  besides Watson know your big game  changers we have this issue electronic  medical records really aren't moving the  needle if you go back to this issue  about the amount of data  that we have and just to give you some  context we have health data on a hundred  million unique individuals from various  points in time right even though we have  36 million health members today and if  you think about our enterprise data  warehouse so we do have Big Data right  we it's bigger than the size of what's  stored in the Library of Congress we  have focused our innovations on how do  we collect and organize all the  information about me not as a member of  a health plan but as an individual  person my daughter went to the  pediatrician had some issues on her her  local tests we had a sender to  Children's Hospital what did I do when I  first got there I had to give all of her  background or history documental that it  was things that we have documentary  history the story that goes on is that  as the pediatrician was calling me to  say call me Monday I was assuming I was  getting the health results having worked  in this industry I simply said to  Children's Hospital do you have an  electronic medical record and can I get  access to the information which I did so  by the time I left Children's Hospital  and got home I had my daughter's lab  results and I pretty much could self  diagnose what the issue was going on and  she had iron deficiency anemia I called  my doctor Monday morning only to tell me  that they were waiting to hear from  Children's Hospital and we're waiting  for the lab results at which to I said  have them would you like them my point  in this simple example is we have to  help facilitate the integration of  information to get people to care they  need in this what we call a longitudinal  patient record I think it's a really  important innovation we'll do mobile  we'll do social we'll do all those kind  of things but you know Watson that can  help us sort through massive amounts of  information unstructured data ability to  aggregate information and if you think  about it as we're talking about new  reimbursement mechanisms part of that  discussion is what data ours and  providers do we need to start bringing  together and does that mean for instance  if your daughter ends up say working  someplace where a WellPoint company  isn't insurer and she's had WellPoint  subsidiaries as  sort of insurer prior to that time where  does that data go this is actually an  issue we're working through now because  as we've started to expose some of our  hospitals physicians to the information  that we have the first question that  comes up is can I have that for all my  patients and so we're actually thinking  through how to think about that model  going forward and and how do we really  make sure you know for us this has to be  a cloud-based solution but obviously  that's something that over time we have  to really think through and do you have  to build that yourself is that not clear  yet we don't have to build the  capabilities ourself in this case we're  really bringing integrating technologies  together and bringing a solution forward  but I believe you know it has to be a  cloud solution it's been really  challenging for delivery systems to  implement electronic medical records  their resources are generally very thin  how do we make something simple to get  access to that's going to be important  as well  so in a place like Microsoft smart house  of the future where you've got sensors  that are feeding data about the  environment and about the people in the  environment to to different systems be  interpreted are those the kinds of  things that will also become  transformative and will then feed into  the data that you're that you want to  gather or that a patient is going to  have what happens with those and do they  transforms things transform things or  are they just sort of nice little  research experiments everything is  becoming censored everything's becoming  wired these days and generating lots of  information I think the key point around  this goes back to I absolutely believe  for it to work it has to naturally fit  into the consumers world right and so I  really think over time we've had  monitoring devices out there for some  time  why haven't they gotten adopted  sometimes it's a challenging to use them  and plug them in technology keeps  getting smaller and smaller it's a  smores law thing and processing faster  and when you start to think about the  capabilities some new innovations that  are starting to emerge an EKG that's  really a case that fits over your  smartphone I mean I've tried it it works  fantastic you know those kind of things  it's not something different I have my  smartphone how can i how can we fit into  those kind of things that's what I like  about telemedicine you know what's  taking the doctor where the consumer is  is the consumers in their home I think  there's lots of information we can think  about we actually do some of this today  in a subsidiary we have called care more  that really focuses on that Medicare  Advantage and we actually have those  seniors actively manage their weight and  we can see that if there is a rapid  increase in weight gain within a  particular day  someone needs to reach out to them  because they may have an accelerating  health condition that we want to  intervene before they end up in the  emergency room but it's got to be simple  and it's got to fit into the consumer  world we have to keep thinking about the  design because that I think is  ultimately what's going to drive the  engagement with a consumer what are the  biggest obstacles organizationally to  transforming your operations I think  this is one of the most transformational  times we'll ever see in this business  and our industry has been slower to  transform I think and a lot of things  have been tried in healthcare so I think  for the industry in general and also  some of the challenges we face is you  know not making sure that we're not  looking at things based on where we've  been but where the market is going and  also making sure we're not just paying  attention to what's happening in our  industry I think there's a huge  intersection as I mentioned earlier with  what consumers are expecting how they're  engaging with technology we have to  capture that I just want to throw a  chronic care question at you in our last  couple minutes here so chronic care  accounts for something like 70 to 75% of  all healthcare spending in this country  do you feel like an insurer can really  sort of change that in some way that's  going to be meaningful yeah I mean when  you really look at what drives the cost  this is the crux of the issue and when  you look behind that there are many  behavioral related items that come into  play are people staying compliant with  their medication are people managing  smoking and weight and many other things  and so this is really a tough issue but  one of the ways that we have to look at  it is not just from a medical  perspective but what are the social and  behavioral elements of solving that  problem  so that goes back to working across all  the people that are giving care not even  in the traditional sense of of a medical  doctor but sometimes they're social  workers involved in other things well we  look at that in our models of managing  Medicaid or Medicare these issues are  incredibly relevant so again we believe  it's around aligning the incentives  bringing all the stakeholders to the  table having the integrated data as well  and understanding that it's a  combination of both the social  behavioral as well as medical issues  that we have to solve to solve that  problem for the long-term so could what  I call that a cautious yes we can change  this problem or we have to change this  problem and I think you know when you  look at our care more model in Medicare  it's demonstrated that we've been able  to reduce readmissions to hospitals  we've demonstrated that we've been able  to reduce the amount of diabetic  amputations real proven facts around  those topics so yes we absolutely have  to yes we will we have to and if we  don't address this chronic care issue  not only for Medicare and Medicaid but  across our our other lines of business  as well  we're not going to get at the ultimate  cost issue well Lori thank you so much  for sharing your insights on digital  transformation whether it's to be here  thank you Michael this is Michael  Fitzgerald with MIT Sloan management  review thank you for joining us for this  discussion on digital transformation and  we look forward to having you join us  for our next one
 

